      "side": 1,
      "message": "nit.\nReplacing `DynamicSet` is a goo shout, we don\u0027t need `DynamicSet`.\n\nHaving said that, I was looking at its implementation and DynamicSet uses `\u003d\u003d` equality in its `contains` method:\n\n```\npublic boolean contains(T item) {\n  Iterator\u003cT\u003e iterator \u003d iterator();\n  while (iterator.hasNext()) {\n    T candidate \u003d iterator.next();\n    if (candidate \u003d\u003d item) {\n      return true;\n    }\n  }\n  return false;\n}\n```\n\n`ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet()` uses `equals()` and `hashCode()` instead.\nThis means two distinct registered filters might now compare equal (when their `equals()` is `true`), even if they are different instances.\n\nThat said, I am not sure how likely/possible it is to register filters with the same hash code, so this is purely theoretical.\nFeel free to ignore.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 69,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 69,
        "endChar": 62
      },
      "revId": "2c86d5f777811eecd3f7881790122eaa47b22cdf",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
